# Spend you can cap

```elixir
Mix.install([{:wardrik, "~> 0.1.0-rc.2"}, {:kino, "~> 0.19"}])
```

## What you'll build

Two agents share acme's ceiling of two cents a day. The writer also has a budget of its own, one
cent a day. You'll watch their spend climb in a live table. The writer stops to ask for more, and
the ceiling refuses the editor outright.

The whole notebook spends about three cents of model calls.

## What a call costs

Wardrik prices each model call from the model catalog, in dollars per million tokens:

```elixir
{:ok, haiku} = ReqLLM.model("anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5")
haiku.cost
```

So a call that reads 400 tokens and writes 700 costs:

```elixir
(400 * haiku.cost.input + 700 * haiku.cost.output) / 1_000_000
```

A budget in dollars refuses a model the catalog has no price for, since it can't count it. Give
one a price with Wardrik's `:prices` option.

## A budget on paper

A budget caps what one agent spends on its model. `WardrikPolicy.Budget.check/2` shows what it
makes of what was spent so far, with no model involved:

```elixir
asks = WardrikPolicy.new!(budget: [usd: 0.01, per: :day]).budget
denies = WardrikPolicy.new!(budget: [usd: 0.01, per: :day, on_exhausted: :deny]).budget

for usd <- [0.004, 0.009, 0.012] do
  %{
    spent: usd,
    asks: WardrikPolicy.Budget.check(asks, %{usd: usd}),
    denies: WardrikPolicy.Budget.check(denies, %{usd: usd})
  }
end
```

The call that crosses the limit runs. The next one does what `on_exhausted` says:

* `:ask` - the default. The call waits for a person. Approving allows the budget's `grant` more,
  another limit's worth unless set.
* `:deny` - the call is refused.

Messages round to the cent.

## Setup

An org's ceilings come from your `Wardrik.Orgs` module. Here acme's agents share two cents a day,
and the ceiling refuses calls past it:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Orgs do
  @behaviour Wardrik.Orgs

  @impl true
  def settings("acme", _config) do
    {:ok,
     %Wardrik.OrgSettings{
       ceilings: %{"plan" => [usd: 0.02, per: :day, on_exhausted: :deny]}
     }}
  end

  def settings(org, _config),
    do: {:error, Wardrik.Error.new(:not_found, "There's no org #{org} here.")}
end

{:ok, _} = Wardrik.start_link(name: MyApp.AgentRuntime, orgs: {MyApp.Orgs, []})

scope =
  Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@maria",
    roles: [:owner],
    resources: ["acme/@maria/*", "acme/ceiling"]
  )
```

`acme/ceiling` lets the scope read the org's ceilings and approve calls past them.

## Two agents

The agents call a model. Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the Secrets panel first.

```elixir
System.put_env("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", System.fetch_env!("LB_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"))

{:ok, _} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(scope, "acme/@maria/writer",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You write short stories about office life.",
    policy: [budget: [usd: 0.01, per: :day]]
  )

{:ok, _} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(scope, "acme/@maria/editor",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You write short memos about office life."
  )
```

The editor has no budget of its own. The ceiling holds it all the same.

## Live spend

`Wardrik.Agents.list/2` returns what each agent spent today. `Wardrik.Ceilings.list/2` returns
what the org spent against each ceiling. Both tables refresh every second:

```elixir
dollars = fn usd -> Float.round(usd / 1, 4) end

agent_rows = fn ->
  {:ok, agents} = Wardrik.Agents.list(scope)

  for agent <- agents do
    %{
      agent: agent.id,
      status: agent.status,
      tokens: agent.spent_today["tokens"],
      usd: dollars.(agent.spent_today["usd"])
    }
  end
end

ceiling_rows = fn ->
  {:ok, ceilings} = Wardrik.Ceilings.list(scope, owner: "acme")

  for spend <- ceilings do
    %{
      ceiling: spend.ceiling,
      limit_usd: spend.limit.usd,
      spent_usd: dollars.(spend.spent.usd),
      spent_tokens: spend.spent.tokens,
      on_exhausted: spend.on_exhausted,
      starts_over: spend.starts_over
    }
  end
end

agents_table =
  Kino.DataTable.new(agent_rows.(), name: "Agents today", keys: [:agent, :status, :tokens, :usd])

ceilings_table =
  Kino.DataTable.new(ceiling_rows.(),
    name: "acme's ceilings",
    keys: [:ceiling, :limit_usd, :spent_usd, :spent_tokens, :on_exhausted, :starts_over]
  )

Kino.listen(1_000, fn _tick ->
  Kino.DataTable.update(agents_table, agent_rows.())
  Kino.DataTable.update(ceilings_table, ceiling_rows.())
end)

Kino.Layout.grid([agents_table, ceilings_table])
```

## The writer's budget asks

Ask the writer for stories until a reply doesn't come back. Keep an eye on the tables above:

```elixir
Enum.reduce_while(1..10, nil, fn n, _ ->
  case Wardrik.Agent.reply(scope, "acme/@maria/writer", "Write story #{n}, in about 300 words.") do
    {:ok, _reply} -> {:cont, n}
    stopped -> {:halt, stopped}
  end
end)
```

The writer crossed its cent, so its next model call waits on a `:model_approval` interrupt. The
writer is `:parked` in the table. To let it finish the story, allow another cent:

```elixir
allow = Kino.Control.button("Allow the writer one cent more")
outcome = Kino.Frame.new()

Kino.listen(allow, fn _click ->
  result = Wardrik.Agent.approve(scope, "acme/@maria/writer", "model")
  Kino.Frame.render(outcome, Kino.Text.new(inspect(result)))
end)

Kino.Layout.grid([allow, outcome])
```

## The org's ceiling refuses

The editor has no budget, but it spends from acme's ceiling with the writer. Ask it for memos:

```elixir
Enum.reduce_while(1..10, nil, fn n, _ ->
  case Wardrik.Agent.reply(scope, "acme/@maria/editor", "Write memo #{n}, in about 300 words.") do
    {:ok, _reply} -> {:cont, n}
    stopped -> {:halt, stopped}
  end
end)
```

Once acme's agents spent the two cents, the next model call of any of them is refused. The run
fails with the ceiling's message. Wardrik also writes `wardrik.ceiling.reached` to the agent's log:

```elixir
{:ok, page} = Wardrik.Agent.events(scope, "acme/@maria/editor")

for %{type: "wardrik.ceiling.reached", data: data} <- page.events, do: data
```

The ceiling holds every agent of the org. Click the writer's button now, and its run fails the
same way.

A ceiling's `on_exhausted` is one of:

* `:ask` - the default. Waits on a `:ceiling_approval` interrupt for someone allowed
  `ceiling.approve` on `acme/ceiling`, as an owner is. Approving allows the ceiling's `grant` more
  to the whole org for that period.
* `:deny` - refuses the call, as here.
* `:continue` - lets it run, so work always carries on. It still writes `wardrik.ceiling.reached`.

A ceiling takes what a budget takes, except `per: :turn`. Its period starts over on the day in the
`starts_over` column.

